    {
      "id": "nyptOwDHyfQYwpvCqZI9H",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 324.4306965977696,
      "y": 18475.646452774046,
      "width": 957,
      "height": 1050,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b58",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 413457095,
      "version": 51,
      "versionNonce": 803770665,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965376427,
      "created": 1789965193112,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Today we'll start moving from design → actual backend APIs.\n\nClient\n   ↓\nHTTP Request\n   ↓\nURL\n   ↓\nDRF View\n   ↓\nAuthentication\n   ↓\nPermission\n   ↓\nSerializer\n   ↓\nService / Business Logic\n   ↓\nDjango ORM\n   ↓\nPostgreSQL\n   ↓\nSerializer\n   ↓\nHTTP Response\n\n\nWe'll learn these step-by-step:\n\n1. HTTP methods — GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE\n2. HTTP status codes\n3. DRF serializers\n4. Validation\n5. APIView vs ViewSet\n6. Authentication & permissions in DRF\n7. Service layer\n8. Error handling\n9. Pagination/filtering\n10. Build actual APIs for our Enterprise Workspace\n\nAnd as before, I'll explain the concept simply first, then we'll do an interview-style \nquestion.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Today we'll start moving from design → actual backend APIs.\n\nClient\n   ↓\nHTTP Request\n   ↓\nURL\n   ↓\nDRF View\n   ↓\nAuthentication\n   ↓\nPermission\n   ↓\nSerializer\n   ↓\nService / Business Logic\n   ↓\nDjango ORM\n   ↓\nPostgreSQL\n   ↓\nSerializer\n   ↓\nHTTP Response\n\n\nWe'll learn these step-by-step:\n\n1. HTTP methods — GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE\n2. HTTP status codes\n3. DRF serializers\n4. Validation\n5. APIView vs ViewSet\n6. Authentication & permissions in DRF\n7. Service layer\n8. Error handling\n9. Pagination/filtering\n10. Build actual APIs for our Enterprise Workspace\n\nAnd as before, I'll explain the concept simply first, then we'll do an interview-style \nquestion.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "0KYDdsj9dVfdndiR7LRAs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 250.4619465977696,
      "y": 19604.665984024046,
      "width": 407,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b59",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1291965897,
      "version": 11,
      "versionNonce": 1823372839,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965442555,
      "created": 1789965425932,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 1\n\nSuppose we want to create a new task.\nWhich HTTP method should we use?\n    POST /api/projects/10/tasks/\nWhy POST and not GET?\nGive me your answer.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 1\n\nSuppose we want to create a new task.\nWhich HTTP method should we use?\n    POST /api/projects/10/tasks/\nWhy POST and not GET?\nGive me your answer.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "l6fYafmLntQhWJiSlUPEX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 297.4580403477696,
      "y": 19812.408171524046,
      "width": 671,
      "height": 1025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5A",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 599435943,
      "version": 50,
      "versionNonce": 551677351,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965602178,
      "created": 1789965510085,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\nExactly.\nPOST is generally used to create a new resource in REST APIs.\nFor our project:\nPOST /api/projects/10/tasks/\n\nmeans: \"Create a new task inside project 10.\"\n\nRequest:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n  \"assigned_to\": 42\n}\n\nThe server creates:\n\nTask\nid = 101\ntitle = \"Fix payment API\"\nproject_id = 10\ncreated_by = request.user\nassigned_to = 42\n\n\nWhy not GET?\n\nGET is intended for retrieving data, not creating it.\nGET /api/projects/10/tasks/\n→ \"Give me the tasks.\"\nPOST /api/projects/10/tasks/\n→ \"Create a task.\"\n\nA simple way to remember:\n| Method   | Typical purpose |\n| -------- | --------------- |\n| `GET`    | Read            |\n| `POST`   | Create          |\n| `PUT`    | Replace/update  |\n| `PATCH`  | Partial update  |\n| `DELETE` | Delete          |\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\nExactly.\nPOST is generally used to create a new resource in REST APIs.\nFor our project:\nPOST /api/projects/10/tasks/\n\nmeans: \"Create a new task inside project 10.\"\n\nRequest:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n  \"assigned_to\": 42\n}\n\nThe server creates:\n\nTask\nid = 101\ntitle = \"Fix payment API\"\nproject_id = 10\ncreated_by = request.user\nassigned_to = 42\n\n\nWhy not GET?\n\nGET is intended for retrieving data, not creating it.\nGET /api/projects/10/tasks/\n→ \"Give me the tasks.\"\nPOST /api/projects/10/tasks/\n→ \"Create a task.\"\n\nA simple way to remember:\n| Method   | Typical purpose |\n| -------- | --------------- |\n| `GET`    | Read            |\n| `POST`   | Create          |\n| `PUT`    | Replace/update  |\n| `PATCH`  | Partial update  |\n| `DELETE` | Delete          |\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "3pRMBmPY-9QoTXucMhwhS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 217.4619465977696,
      "y": 20901.662077774046,
      "width": 627,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1885952775,
      "version": 20,
      "versionNonce": 1230746281,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965654628,
      "created": 1789965630618,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 2 — PUT vs PATCH 🔥\nWe have this task:\n{\n  \"id\": 101,\n  \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n  \"description\": \"Fix timeout issue\",\n  \"priority\": \"HIGH\",\n  \"status\": \"TODO\"\n}\n\nNow Rahul only wants to change: priority: HIGH → CRITICAL\n\nWhich HTTP method should we generally use?\n\nA) POST\nB) GET\nC) PUT\nD) PATCH",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 2 — PUT vs PATCH 🔥\nWe have this task:\n{\n  \"id\": 101,\n  \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n  \"description\": \"Fix timeout issue\",\n  \"priority\": \"HIGH\",\n  \"status\": \"TODO\"\n}\n\nNow Rahul only wants to change: priority: HIGH → CRITICAL\n\nWhich HTTP method should we generally use?\n\nA) POST\nB) GET\nC) PUT\nD) PATCH",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "2HBaioMzNSsRWkX_XdD_X",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 268.3369465977696,
      "y": 21401.662077774046,
      "width": 1034,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 279340873,
      "version": 37,
      "versionNonce": 573567303,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965785358,
      "created": 1789965701235,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\nPATCH is used when we want to make a partial update to an existing resource.\nHere:\n\nPATCH /api/tasks/101/\n{\n  \"priority\": \"CRITICAL\"\n}\n\nOnly priority changes. The other fields remain unchanged:\ntitle       → Fix payment API       ✅ unchanged\ndescription → Fix timeout issue     ✅ unchanged\npriority    → CRITICAL              🔄 changed\nstatus      → TODO                  ✅ unchanged\n\nPUT vs PATCH — remember this\nPUT → generally means replace the resource representation.\n\nPUT /api/tasks/101/\n{\n  \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n  \"description\": \"Fix timeout issue\",\n  \"priority\": \"CRITICAL\",\n  \"status\": \"TODO\"\n}\n\n🎯 Interview answer\n\nPATCH is used for partial updates, where only specific fields of an existing resource need to \nbe modified. PUT is generally used when replacing the complete resource representation.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\nPATCH is used when we want to make a partial update to an existing resource.\nHere:\n\nPATCH /api/tasks/101/\n{\n  \"priority\": \"CRITICAL\"\n}\n\nOnly priority changes. The other fields remain unchanged:\ntitle       → Fix payment API       ✅ unchanged\ndescription → Fix timeout issue     ✅ unchanged\npriority    → CRITICAL              🔄 changed\nstatus      → TODO                  ✅ unchanged\n\nPUT vs PATCH — remember this\nPUT → generally means replace the resource representation.\n\nPUT /api/tasks/101/\n{\n  \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n  \"description\": \"Fix timeout issue\",\n  \"priority\": \"CRITICAL\",\n  \"status\": \"TODO\"\n}\n\n🎯 Interview answer\n\nPATCH is used for partial updates, where only specific fields of an existing resource need to \nbe modified. PUT is generally used when replacing the complete resource representation.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "g00aeiRh10eKejEA5EXWU",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 176.3369465977696,
      "y": 22220.271452774046,
      "width": 583,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5D",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1963885223,
      "version": 11,
      "versionNonce": 1373899847,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965808944,
      "created": 1789965794153,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 3 — HTTP Status Codes\nRahul sends: POST /api/projects/10/tasks/\nThe task is successfully created.\n\nWhich HTTP status code should our API ideally return?\n\nA) 200 OK\nB) 201 Created\nC) 204 No Content\nD) 400 Bad Request",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 3 — HTTP Status Codes\nRahul sends: POST /api/projects/10/tasks/\nThe task is successfully created.\n\nWhich HTTP status code should our API ideally return?\n\nA) 200 OK\nB) 201 Created\nC) 204 No Content\nD) 400 Bad Request",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "txxsy92Yc6gSBSIvMMzBo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 233.3369465977696,
      "y": 22496.787077774046,
      "width": 1045,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5F",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 215252391,
      "version": 24,
      "versionNonce": 1964783943,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965967346,
      "created": 1789965849086,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\n201 Created is the appropriate status for successfully creating a new resource.\n\nQuick status-code map\nKeep this mental model:\n\n2xx → Success\n4xx → Client/request problem\n5xx → Server problem\n\nCommon ones:\n\n| Status | Meaning                   | Example                        |\n| ------ | ------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `200`  | Success                   | GET task                       |\n| `201`  | Created                   | POST task                      |\n| `204`  | Success, no response body | DELETE task                    |\n| `400`  | Invalid request           | Bad input                      |\n| `401`  | Not authenticated         | Missing/invalid JWT            |\n| `403`  | Not permitted             | User lacks permission          |\n| `404`  | Resource not found        | Task doesn't exist             |\n| `409`  | Conflict                  | Duplicate/conflicting resource |\n| `500`  | Server error              | Unexpected backend failure     |\n\n\n🎯 Interview answer\n\nWe return 201 Created when a POST request successfully creates a new resource. It communicates \nto the client that a new resource was successfully created.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\n201 Created is the appropriate status for successfully creating a new resource.\n\nQuick status-code map\nKeep this mental model:\n\n2xx → Success\n4xx → Client/request problem\n5xx → Server problem\n\nCommon ones:\n\n| Status | Meaning                   | Example                        |\n| ------ | ------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `200`  | Success                   | GET task                       |\n| `201`  | Created                   | POST task                      |\n| `204`  | Success, no response body | DELETE task                    |\n| `400`  | Invalid request           | Bad input                      |\n| `401`  | Not authenticated         | Missing/invalid JWT            |\n| `403`  | Not permitted             | User lacks permission          |\n| `404`  | Resource not found        | Task doesn't exist             |\n| `409`  | Conflict                  | Duplicate/conflicting resource |\n| `500`  | Server error              | Unexpected backend failure     |\n\n\n🎯 Interview answer\n\nWe return 201 Created when a POST request successfully creates a new resource. It communicates \nto the client that a new resource was successfully created.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "DP1-FdIDIke6F3RDdAdYA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 147.4502278477696,
      "y": 23313.544890274046,
      "width": 451,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1761594023,
      "version": 21,
      "versionNonce": 2056663785,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789966060403,
      "created": 1789966001187,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 4 — 400 vs 401 🔥\nRahul sends: POST /api/projects/10/tasks/\n\nbut sends an invalid request:\n{\n  \"title\": \"\"\n}\nThe API requires a non-empty title.\nWhich status should we return?\nA) 400 Bad Request\nB) 401 Unauthorized\nC) 403 Forbidden\nD) 500 Internal Server Error",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 4 — 400 vs 401 🔥\nRahul sends: POST /api/projects/10/tasks/\n\nbut sends an invalid request:\n{\n  \"title\": \"\"\n}\nThe API requires a non-empty title.\nWhich status should we return?\nA) 400 Bad Request\nB) 401 Unauthorized\nC) 403 Forbidden\nD) 500 Internal Server Error",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "sF4CKSz2_RxQjbanfKGYP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 194.4541340977696,
      "y": 23666.654265274046,
      "width": 979,
      "height": 975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1017443945,
      "version": 41,
      "versionNonce": 1133322087,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789966151196,
      "created": 1789966061753,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\nA) 400 Bad Request\nThe user is authenticated, but the request data is invalid.\n\n{\n  \"title\": \"\"\n}\n\nOur validation rule says:\ntitle must not be empty\n\nSo: 400 Bad Request\n\nCompare these carefully\n401 → Who are you?\n      Authentication problem\n\n403 → I know who you are,\n      but you're not allowed\n\n400 → I understand your request,\n      but the data/request is invalid\n\nExample:\n\nNo JWT\n       → 401\n\nValid JWT + no permission\n       → 403\n\nValid JWT + title = \"\"\n       → 400\n\n🎯 Interview-quality answer\n\nWe return 400 Bad Request when the client sends invalid input that fails API validation. \nA 401 is for authentication failure, while 403 is for an authenticated user who lacks \npermission.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\nA) 400 Bad Request\nThe user is authenticated, but the request data is invalid.\n\n{\n  \"title\": \"\"\n}\n\nOur validation rule says:\ntitle must not be empty\n\nSo: 400 Bad Request\n\nCompare these carefully\n401 → Who are you?\n      Authentication problem\n\n403 → I know who you are,\n      but you're not allowed\n\n400 → I understand your request,\n      but the data/request is invalid\n\nExample:\n\nNo JWT\n       → 401\n\nValid JWT + no permission\n       → 403\n\nValid JWT + title = \"\"\n       → 400\n\n🎯 Interview-quality answer\n\nWe return 400 Bad Request when the client sends invalid input that fails API validation. \nA 401 is for authentication failure, while 403 is for an authenticated user who lacks \npermission.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "mlPyikTBgU2w4QztlGF2R",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 89.21194659776961,
      "y": 24728.908171524046,
      "width": 1122,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1654973639,
      "version": 20,
      "versionNonce": 288783433,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789966244510,
      "created": 1789966160856,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 5 — Now DRF starts 🚀\nImagine our API receives:\n\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 42\n}\n\nBefore saving this data to the database, we need to:\n\nCheck title is present\nCheck priority is valid\nCheck assigned_to exists\nCheck the assigned user has access to the project\nConvert/validate incoming data\nReturn useful validation errors\n\nWhich DRF component should primarily handle request data validation and serialization/deserialization?\n\nA) Model\nB) Serializer\nC) URL\nD) Database",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 5 — Now DRF starts 🚀\nImagine our API receives:\n\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 42\n}\n\nBefore saving this data to the database, we need to:\n\nCheck title is present\nCheck priority is valid\nCheck assigned_to exists\nCheck the assigned user has access to the project\nConvert/validate incoming data\nReturn useful validation errors\n\nWhich DRF component should primarily handle request data validation and serialization/deserialization?\n\nA) Model\nB) Serializer\nC) URL\nD) Database",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "d-RrM3OdaRtgtCxHxmm5i",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 137.9072590977696,
      "y": 25386.412077774046,
      "width": 1089,
      "height": 2125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5K",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1636177129,
      "version": 65,
      "versionNonce": 772344233,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789966405157,
      "created": 1789966269889,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\nB) Serializer\nThis is one of the most important concepts in Django REST Framework.\nA serializer acts like a translator + validator between:\nJSON / Python data\n       ↕\nDjango objects / database\n\nFor our Task API\n\nClient sends:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 42\n}\n\nThe serializer can validate:\ntitle\n  ↓\nIs it present?\nIs it valid?\n\npriority\n  ↓\nIs HIGH an allowed value?\n\nassigned_to\n  ↓\nDoes user 42 exist?\n\nThen:\n\nJSON\n ↓\nSerializer\n ↓\nvalidated_data\n ↓\nService / View\n ↓\nORM\n ↓\nDatabase\n\n\nSerializer also works in the opposite direction\nWhen the database has:\n\nTask\nid = 101\ntitle = \"Fix payment API\"\npriority = \"HIGH\"\n\nwe need to return JSON:\n{\n    \"id\": 101,\n    \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\"\n}\n\nThe serializer can convert the Django object into a representation suitable for the API \nresponse.\n\nSo remember:\n\nRequest JSON\n     ↓\nDESERIALIZATION\n     ↓\nPython / validated data\n     ↓\nDatabase\n\nDatabase\n     ↓\nSERIALIZATION\n     ↓\nResponse JSON\n\n🎯 Interview-quality answer\n\nA DRF serializer is responsible for converting complex Django/Python objects into API-friendly \nrepresentations and validating/deserializing incoming request data. It provides a boundary between \nexternal API data and our application's internal data.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Your answer: ✅ Correct — 10/10\nB) Serializer\nThis is one of the most important concepts in Django REST Framework.\nA serializer acts like a translator + validator between:\nJSON / Python data\n       ↕\nDjango objects / database\n\nFor our Task API\n\nClient sends:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 42\n}\n\nThe serializer can validate:\ntitle\n  ↓\nIs it present?\nIs it valid?\n\npriority\n  ↓\nIs HIGH an allowed value?\n\nassigned_to\n  ↓\nDoes user 42 exist?\n\nThen:\n\nJSON\n ↓\nSerializer\n ↓\nvalidated_data\n ↓\nService / View\n ↓\nORM\n ↓\nDatabase\n\n\nSerializer also works in the opposite direction\nWhen the database has:\n\nTask\nid = 101\ntitle = \"Fix payment API\"\npriority = \"HIGH\"\n\nwe need to return JSON:\n{\n    \"id\": 101,\n    \"title\": \"Fix payment API\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\"\n}\n\nThe serializer can convert the Django object into a representation suitable for the API \nresponse.\n\nSo remember:\n\nRequest JSON\n     ↓\nDESERIALIZATION\n     ↓\nPython / validated data\n     ↓\nDatabase\n\nDatabase\n     ↓\nSERIALIZATION\n     ↓\nResponse JSON\n\n🎯 Interview-quality answer\n\nA DRF serializer is responsible for converting complex Django/Python objects into API-friendly \nrepresentations and validating/deserializing incoming request data. It provides a boundary between \nexternal API data and our application's internal data.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "8Uk7peItNDL-ZdHFc2rri",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 70.18460284776961,
      "y": 27575.900359024046,
      "width": 1694,
      "height": 575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 660357297,
      "version": 25,
      "versionNonce": 899252977,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790307116847,
      "created": 1790307064299,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q6 — Serializer vs Business Logic\nSuppose we have this API: POST /projects/10/tasks/\nRequest:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix login bug\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 25\n}\n\nThe serializer already checks:\n\ntitle is required\npriority is valid\nassigned_to exists\n\nBut now we need to check:\nIs Rahul actually allowed to create a task in Project 10?\nWhere should this authorization/business rule primarily live?\n\nA) Yes, all business logic should go into serializers\nB) No, serializers mainly handle representation/validation; authorization and business logic should be handled in the appropriate permission/service layer\nC) Yes, because serializers can access the database\nD) Put everything in the Django model",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q6 — Serializer vs Business Logic\nSuppose we have this API: POST /projects/10/tasks/\nRequest:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix login bug\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 25\n}\n\nThe serializer already checks:\n\ntitle is required\npriority is valid\nassigned_to exists\n\nBut now we need to check:\nIs Rahul actually allowed to create a task in Project 10?\nWhere should this authorization/business rule primarily live?\n\nA) Yes, all business logic should go into serializers\nB) No, serializers mainly handle representation/validation; authorization and business logic should be handled in the appropriate permission/service layer\nC) Yes, because serializers can access the database\nD) Put everything in the Django model",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ngCACzj__2MBRkW_IvVEn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 309.9502278477696,
      "y": 27555.544890274046,
      "width": 11,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1037805663,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1517453521,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790307077482,
      "created": 1790307077116,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "jRZRJ2e3V_HHy2DM24I9H",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 118.45022784776961,
      "y": 28167.412077774046,
      "width": 1078,
      "height": 1775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 20954257,
      "version": 66,
      "versionNonce": 1788137119,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790307348376,
      "created": 1790307131603,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — B\nYour understanding is right.\nWhy?\nA serializer's primary job is:\n\nJSON\n ↓\nValidate data\n ↓\nPython / validated_data\n\nFor example:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix login bug\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\"\n}\n\nSerializer checks things like:\n\nIs title present?\nIs priority valid?\nIs assigned_to a valid user ID?\nIs the data in the correct format?\n\nBut:\n\"Is this user allowed to create a task in this project?\" is an authorization/business rule.\n\nA cleaner architecture is:\nRequest\n   ↓\nAuthentication\n   ↓\nPermission\n   ↓\nSerializer\n   ↓\nService / Business Logic\n   ↓\nDatabase\n\nFor example:\nif not user_can_create_task(request.user, project):\n    raise PermissionDenied()\n\nImportant interview nuance\nDon't say:\n\"Serializers should NEVER contain business logic.\"\n\nThat's too absolute.\nSerializers can perform some data-dependent validation.\nFor example:\n\ndef validate(self, data):\n    if data[\"start_date\"] > data[\"end_date\"]:\n        raise ValidationError(\"Invalid dates\")\n    return data\n\nThat's reasonable validation.\nBut authorization such as:\n\nCan Amit create a task in Project X?\nCan Amit assign Rahul to this task?\nCan Amit delete this project?\n\nshould generally be handled through permissions and/or the service/domain layer.\n\nInterview-quality answer\n\n\"DRF serializers should primarily handle serialization and input validation. Authorization should \nbe handled by permission classes, while complex business rules should generally live in a service \nor domain layer. Serializers can still perform data-level validation where appropriate.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — B\nYour understanding is right.\nWhy?\nA serializer's primary job is:\n\nJSON\n ↓\nValidate data\n ↓\nPython / validated_data\n\nFor example:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix login bug\",\n    \"priority\": \"HIGH\"\n}\n\nSerializer checks things like:\n\nIs title present?\nIs priority valid?\nIs assigned_to a valid user ID?\nIs the data in the correct format?\n\nBut:\n\"Is this user allowed to create a task in this project?\" is an authorization/business rule.\n\nA cleaner architecture is:\nRequest\n   ↓\nAuthentication\n   ↓\nPermission\n   ↓\nSerializer\n   ↓\nService / Business Logic\n   ↓\nDatabase\n\nFor example:\nif not user_can_create_task(request.user, project):\n    raise PermissionDenied()\n\nImportant interview nuance\nDon't say:\n\"Serializers should NEVER contain business logic.\"\n\nThat's too absolute.\nSerializers can perform some data-dependent validation.\nFor example:\n\ndef validate(self, data):\n    if data[\"start_date\"] > data[\"end_date\"]:\n        raise ValidationError(\"Invalid dates\")\n    return data\n\nThat's reasonable validation.\nBut authorization such as:\n\nCan Amit create a task in Project X?\nCan Amit assign Rahul to this task?\nCan Amit delete this project?\n\nshould generally be handled through permissions and/or the service/domain layer.\n\nInterview-quality answer\n\n\"DRF serializers should primarily handle serialization and input validation. Authorization should \nbe handled by permission classes, while complex business rules should generally live in a service \nor domain layer. Serializers can still perform data-level validation where appropriate.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "WYM0B-Z1Qy_bv4IGBDXZb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 47.82522784776961,
      "y": 29994.294890274046,
      "width": 770,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 385302271,
      "version": 16,
      "versionNonce": 1479651697,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790307415834,
      "created": 1790307362905,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q7 — Authentication vs Permission\nImagine this request:\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\nAuthorization: Bearer <JWT>\n\nThe JWT is valid, so we know the user is logged in.\nBut the user is not a member of the organization that owns Project 10.\nWhat should happen?\nA) Allow the request because the JWT is valid\nB) Return 401 Unauthorized\nC) Return 403 Forbidden\nD) Return 201 Created",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q7 — Authentication vs Permission\nImagine this request:\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\nAuthorization: Bearer <JWT>\n\nThe JWT is valid, so we know the user is logged in.\nBut the user is not a member of the organization that owns Project 10.\nWhat should happen?\nA) Allow the request because the JWT is valid\nB) Return 401 Unauthorized\nC) Return 403 Forbidden\nD) Return 201 Created",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "LRQyLLF99FWd0uSmNSjzf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 89.82522784776961,
      "y": 30317.544890274046,
      "width": 1056,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5P",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1068006161,
      "version": 13,
      "versionNonce": 1120364753,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790307509693,
      "created": 1790307473209,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "403 Forbidden means:\n\"I know who you are, but you're not allowed to perform this action.\"\n\nAmit → valid JWT ✅\nAmit → not member of Organization X ❌\n       ↓\n      403\n\nOne nuance: for cross-tenant resources, some systems intentionally return 404 instead of 403 to \navoid revealing that the resource exists. But for this question, 403 is the expected answer.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "403 Forbidden means:\n\"I know who you are, but you're not allowed to perform this action.\"\n\nAmit → valid JWT ✅\nAmit → not member of Organization X ❌\n       ↓\n      403\n\nOne nuance: for cross-tenant resources, some systems intentionally return 404 instead of 403 to \navoid revealing that the resource exists. But for this question, 403 is the expected answer.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "1aHqJ2nK6eF1NAyodj7CG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 30.57522784776961,
      "y": 30664.790984024046,
      "width": 891,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5Q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 94170737,
      "version": 49,
      "versionNonce": 170896977,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790308008461,
      "created": 1790307871796,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q8 — DRF Permission\nImagine:\nOrganization: Google\nProject: Payment System\n\nAmit sends:\n\"Create a task in Payment System.\"\n\nBefore creating the task, the backend needs to ask:\n\"Is Amit a member of Google?\"\n\nIf yes → allow.\nIf no → reject.\n\nNow the question is:\nWhere should Django/DRF check this permission?\n\n\nA) Model\nThe Task model mainly describes what a Task looks like:\n\nB) DRF Permission class\nThis is specifically designed for questions like:\n\"Is this user allowed to perform this action?\"\n\nC) Serializer\nSerializer mainly asks:\n\"Is the data valid?\"\n\nD) Frontend\nNever rely on the frontend for security.\nEven if the UI hides the \"Create Task\" button, someone can directly call the API.\n\nSo the question in very simple words is:\nWhere should we check whether a user is allowed to perform an API action?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q8 — DRF Permission\nImagine:\nOrganization: Google\nProject: Payment System\n\nAmit sends:\n\"Create a task in Payment System.\"\n\nBefore creating the task, the backend needs to ask:\n\"Is Amit a member of Google?\"\n\nIf yes → allow.\nIf no → reject.\n\nNow the question is:\nWhere should Django/DRF check this permission?\n\n\nA) Model\nThe Task model mainly describes what a Task looks like:\n\nB) DRF Permission class\nThis is specifically designed for questions like:\n\"Is this user allowed to perform this action?\"\n\nC) Serializer\nSerializer mainly asks:\n\"Is the data valid?\"\n\nD) Frontend\nNever rely on the frontend for security.\nEven if the UI hides the \"Create Task\" button, someone can directly call the API.\n\nSo the question in very simple words is:\nWhere should we check whether a user is allowed to perform an API action?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "4wj9UaMHhClJDZBQ5mSY5",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 90.23538409776961,
      "y": 31554.095671524046,
      "width": 1210,
      "height": 1425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5R",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1242660305,
      "version": 36,
      "versionNonce": 1687082751,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790308073167,
      "created": 1790308016764,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "B) DRF Permission class\nYou got it.\nRemember this separation\n\nAuthentication\n    ↓\n\"Who are you?\"\n    ↓\nJWT\n\n\nPermission / Authorization\n    ↓\n\"Are you allowed?\"\n    ↓\nDRF Permission class\n\n\nSerializer\n    ↓\n\"Is the data valid?\"\n\n\nService layer\n    ↓\n\"How should the business operation work?\"\n\n\nOur Task API\n\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\n          ↓\n      JWT valid?\n          ↓\n        YES\n          ↓\n   Permission check\n          ↓\n   Member of Org?\n      ↙       ↘\n    YES        NO\n     ↓          ↓\nSerializer      403\n     ↓\nService\n     ↓\nDatabase\n\n\nThis separation is very important for your Enterprise Workspace project because we'll have many rules such as:\n\nIs user a member of the organization?\nIs user a member of the team?\nCan user create a task?\nCan user assign someone to a task?\nCan user delete a project?\nCan user access a document?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "B) DRF Permission class\nYou got it.\nRemember this separation\n\nAuthentication\n    ↓\n\"Who are you?\"\n    ↓\nJWT\n\n\nPermission / Authorization\n    ↓\n\"Are you allowed?\"\n    ↓\nDRF Permission class\n\n\nSerializer\n    ↓\n\"Is the data valid?\"\n\n\nService layer\n    ↓\n\"How should the business operation work?\"\n\n\nOur Task API\n\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\n          ↓\n      JWT valid?\n          ↓\n        YES\n          ↓\n   Permission check\n          ↓\n   Member of Org?\n      ↙       ↘\n    YES        NO\n     ↓          ↓\nSerializer      403\n     ↓\nService\n     ↓\nDatabase\n\n\nThis separation is very important for your Enterprise Workspace project because we'll have many rules such as:\n\nIs user a member of the organization?\nIs user a member of the team?\nCan user create a task?\nCan user assign someone to a task?\nCan user delete a project?\nCan user access a document?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "U7TwW3F8GtEsZuzAQ3lK3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 19.18850909776961,
      "y": 33058.665984024046,
      "width": 660,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5S",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 696623967,
      "version": 20,
      "versionNonce": 569530481,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790308179791,
      "created": 1790308153438,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q9 — One step deeper\nSuppose Amit is authenticated and is an organization member.\nBut there are different roles:\n\nOrganization\n├── Amit   → ADMIN\n├── Rahul  → MEMBER\n└── Neha   → VIEWER\n\nRequirement:\nOnly ADMIN can delete an organization.\n\n\nWhere should we check this?\n\nA) Serializer\nB) DRF Permission / Authorization layer\nC) Frontend\nD) Database model",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q9 — One step deeper\nSuppose Amit is authenticated and is an organization member.\nBut there are different roles:\n\nOrganization\n├── Amit   → ADMIN\n├── Rahul  → MEMBER\n└── Neha   → VIEWER\n\nRequirement:\nOnly ADMIN can delete an organization.\n\n\nWhere should we check this?\n\nA) Serializer\nB) DRF Permission / Authorization layer\nC) Frontend\nD) Database model",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "X6iPCds1hfYan9Zs_hKC-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 84.95022784776961,
      "y": 33549.787077774046,
      "width": 1144,
      "height": 1150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5T",
